Do not declare ddxInputThreadInit when the input thread is disabled.

include/os.h declares it unconditionally, but hw/xfree86/common/xf86Init.c
defines it inside #if INPUTTHREAD.  hw/xfree86/sdksyms.c is generated by
running the SDK headers through the preprocessor and taking the address of
every _X_EXPORT symbol, so the declaration alone is enough to put a reference
in sdksyms.o -- and linking Xorg then fails on an undefined symbol.

That makes --disable-input-thread unbuildable, which is not the intent of the
option.  Moving the declaration under the same guard as the definition is the
whole fix.

The input thread is off here because errno is a plain global in this libc, not
thread-local; a second thread inside the server would race on it.

--- a/include/os.h
+++ b/include/os.h
@@ -556,8 +556,16 @@
 
 extern _X_EXPORT void
 ddxGiveUp(enum ExitCode error);
+/* Declared only when it is also defined.  hw/xfree86/sdksyms.c is generated by
+ * preprocessing the SDK headers and taking the address of every _X_EXPORT
+ * symbol, so an unguarded declaration here puts a reference to a function that
+ * hw/xfree86/common/xf86Init.c compiles out -- and the Xorg link then fails on
+ * an undefined ddxInputThreadInit, in a configuration --disable-input-thread
+ * is supposed to support. */
+#if INPUTTHREAD
 extern _X_EXPORT void
 ddxInputThreadInit(void);
+#endif
 extern _X_EXPORT int
 TimeSinceLastInputEvent(void);
 
